AB  - We developed the synthetic aperture radar (SAR) coastal flooding mapping network (SARCFMNet) based on deep convolution neural networks (DCNN) to extract coastal inundation from multi-temporal and dual-polarimetric SAR data with good and robust performance. Two specially-designed adaptations for SAR image flooding mapping are included in the SARCFMNet model: (1) radar remote sensing physics-driven input information design; and (2) regularization suitable for fully convolutional networks. There are two cases presented in this chapter. First, the developed SARCFMNet is trained and evaluated using a dataset derived from 2017 Hurricane Harvey-influenced Houston, Texas, United States. Six image pairs, with ground truth delineated by human with the help of Google Earth and OpenStreetMap, are used to test the proposed SARCFMNet model. The average mapping accuracy and F1 score are 0.98 and 0.88, respectively. They are better than the benchmarking deep learning model for pixel-level classification. This verifies the usefulness of the proposed designs. Geospatial and temporal analyses of 2017 Hurricane Harvey-induced coastal inundation are performed based on the mapping results. Second, the trained and verified SARCFMNet model is applied to Bangladesh, which is one of the United Nations-defined least developed countries, to get nation-level, multi-year, high-temporal-resolution flooding maps. This can help us get deeper understanding of the flooding mechanism of this country. In addition, the impact of meteorological factors in DCNN-based flooding mapping models with possible solutions and cost-sensitive losses are discussed. We also suggest this model can be easily and readily generalized to other multi-temporal ocean remote sensing imagery information mining problems.
