AB  - para>Remote sensing image change detection is a task of identifying and obtaining significant differences between remote sensing images from different periods in the same region. At present, methods based on deep neural networks have excellent performance in the field of computer vision, but their performance is attributed to training a large number of labeled datasets. However, annotating a large number of remote sensing image labels is labor-intensive and expensive. In order to effectively mine useful information from unlabeled data, we propose a generative adversarial semi supervised learning method based on self training and manifold regularization methods for building change detection in large-scale remote sensing images. More specifically, the model first trains the initial segmentation performance with a small amount of labeled data, and then uses self training methods to pseudo label the input unlabeled images. Pseudo labels are only retained when the corresponding class probability has high confidence. In order to enhance the contextual information in image segmentation, manifold regularization is introduced in the training process of image segmentation to improve the segmentation accuracy of the original segmentation model. Therefore, this method mainly trains the network by minimizing the combination of three types of losses, including standard supervised loss on labeled data, self trained mask cross entropy loss on unlabeled data, and manifold regularization loss. We evaluated the proposed semi supervised deep learning method on the WHU-CD dataset, and experimental results showed that this method outperforms other semi supervised methods in building change detection tasks.
